# Arch Linux Installation in VirtualBox

## 1. Introduction

I manually installed Arch Linux in VirtualBox using the official Arch Linux Installation Guide.

I installed LXQt, configured user accounts, enabled SSH, customized Zsh, and installed software from the AUR.

## 2. Virtual Machine Setup

I created a VirtualBox VM with:

- OS: Arch Linux (64-bit)
- Disk: 32 GB
- Firmware: UEFI
- Network: NAT
- Installation media: Arch Linux ISO

I booted into the Arch Linux installation environment.

## 3. Network and Disk Setup

```bash
# Check internet connection
ping -c 3 archlinux.org

# Check system time
timedatectl

# View available disks
lsblk

# Open disk partitioning tool
cfdisk /dev/sda
```

I selected GPT and created two partitions:

| Partition | Size | Format |
|---|---|---|
| /dev/sda1 | 1 GB | FAT32 (EFI) |
| /dev/sda2 | Remaining space | ext4 (root) |

```bash
# Format EFI partition
mkfs.fat -F 32 /dev/sda1

# Format root partition
mkfs.ext4 /dev/sda2

# Mount root filesystem
mount /dev/sda2 /mnt

# Create EFI mount directory
mkdir -p /mnt/boot

# Mount EFI partition
mount /dev/sda1 /mnt/boot
```

## 4. Install Arch Linux

```bash
# Install base system and required packages
pacstrap -K /mnt base linux linux-firmware sudo nano vim networkmanager grub efibootmgr zsh openssh man-db man-pages

# Generate filesystem mount configuration
genfstab -U /mnt >> /mnt/etc/fstab

# Enter installed system
arch-chroot /mnt
```

## 5. Configure the System

```bash
# Set timezone
ln -sf /usr/share/zoneinfo/America/Chicago /etc/localtime

# Set hardware clock
hwclock --systohc

# Edit locale settings
nano /etc/locale.gen
```

I uncommented `en_US.UTF-8 UTF-8`.

```bash
# Generate locale
locale-gen

# Set system language
echo "LANG=en_US.UTF-8" > /etc/locale.conf

# Set hostname
echo "arch-vm" > /etc/hostname

# Edit host mappings
nano /etc/hosts

# Set root password
passwd
```

In `/etc/hosts`, I added:

```text
127.0.0.1 localhost
::1 localhost
127.0.1.1 arch-vm.localdomain arch-vm
```

## 6. Install GRUB Bootloader

```bash
# Install bootloader packages
pacman -S grub efibootmgr

# Install GRUB for UEFI
grub-install --target=x86_64-efi --efi-directory=/boot --bootloader-id=GRUB

# Generate GRUB configuration
grub-mkconfig -o /boot/grub/grub.cfg
```

## 7. Create Users and Configure Sudo

I created two users: `willg` and `codi`.

```bash
# Create primary user with Zsh
useradd -m -G wheel -s /bin/zsh willg

# Set primary user password
passwd willg

# Create second user with Zsh
useradd -m -G wheel -s /bin/zsh codi

# Set second user password
passwd codi

# Edit sudo permissions
EDITOR=nano visudo
```

I uncommented:

```text
%wheel ALL=(ALL:ALL) ALL
```

This allows members of the `wheel` group to use sudo.

## 8. Enable Networking and SSH

```bash
# Enable network management
systemctl enable NetworkManager

# Enable SSH server
systemctl enable sshd
```

## 9. Install LXQt Desktop

```bash
# Update packages
pacman -Syu

# Install desktop, login manager, terminal, and browser
pacman -S xorg-server lxqt sddm breeze-icons qterminal firefox

# Install VirtualBox guest tools
pacman -S virtualbox-guest-utils

# Enable graphical login
systemctl enable sddm

# Boot into graphical mode by default
systemctl set-default graphical.target

# Enable VirtualBox guest service
systemctl enable vboxservice
```

## 10. Reboot into Arch Linux

```bash
# Exit installed system
exit

# Unmount partitions
umount -R /mnt

# Restart virtual machine
reboot
```

I removed the installation ISO and successfully logged into the LXQt desktop.

## 11. Configure Zsh

I switched to my user account and created a Zsh configuration file.

```bash
# Switch to primary user
su - willg

# Create configuration file
touch ~/.zshrc

# Edit configuration
nano ~/.zshrc
```

I added:

```bash
# Enable terminal colors
autoload -Uz colors && colors

# Colored prompt
PROMPT='%F{cyan}%n@%m%f:%F{green}%~%f %# '

# Custom aliases
alias ll='ls -lah --color=auto'
alias update='sudo pacman -Syu'
alias install='sudo pacman -S'
alias search='pacman -Ss'
alias cls='clear'
alias ports='ss -tuln'
alias myip='ip -br addr'
alias ..='cd ..'
alias ...='cd ../..'
```

```bash
# Apply Zsh configuration
source ~/.zshrc
```

The terminal displayed a colored prompt and the aliases worked.

## 12. Install AUR Helper and Package

After logging into LXQt, I opened QTerminal.

```bash
# Update system
sudo pacman -Syu

# Install development tools
sudo pacman -S --needed git base-devel

# Download Yay
cd ~
git clone https://aur.archlinux.org/yay.git

# Enter Yay directory
cd yay

# Inspect build instructions
less PKGBUILD

# Build and install Yay
makepkg -si

# Install Visual Studio Code from AUR
yay -S visual-studio-code-bin
```

Visual Studio Code was successfully installed.

## 13. Problems and Solutions

**Zsh setup screen:** Zsh displayed its first-time configuration menu. I created `.zshrc` manually.

**CPU soft-lockup warning:** A temporary VirtualBox CPU warning appeared during installation. The installation continued.

**Missing vconsole.conf:** Arch displayed a warning about the missing console configuration file. Default settings were used.

## 14. Conclusion

I successfully installed Arch Linux manually in VirtualBox with:

- LXQt graphical desktop
- Two users with sudo permissions
- Zsh with colored terminal and aliases
- NetworkManager and SSH
- GRUB bootloader
- Yay AUR helper
- Visual Studio Code from the AUR

